iv k + 2 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 3)\n       \\]\n     - Simplifying, if \\( k + 2 \\equiv 0 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 3) \\), then \\( k \\equiv 1 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 3) \\).\n\n5. **Case 2:** \\( N \\equiv 2 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 3) \\)\n   - Considering \\( d \\equiv 1 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 3) \\):\n     - When \\( d \\) is appended twice, the new number is:\n       \\[\n       Ndd = 100k + 10d + d \\equiv k + 2 + 2 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 3) \\equiv k + 4 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 3)\n       \\]\n     - Simplifying \\( k + 4 \\):\n       \\[\n       k + 4 \\equiv k + 1 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 3)\n       \\]\n   - Similarly, when \\( d \\equiv 2 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 3) \\):\n     \\[\n     Ndd = 100k + 10d + d \\equiv k + 2 + 2 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 3) \\equiv k + 4 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 3)\n     \\]\n     - Simplifying \\( k + 4 \\):\n       \\[\n       k + 4 \\equiv k + 1 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 3)\n       \\]\n\n6. **Conclusion:**\n   - By appending the digit \\( d \\), twice to the number \\( N \\), we can achieve:\n     \\[\n     Ndd \\equiv k + 3 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 3) \\equiv 0 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 3)\n     \\]\n\nThus, the minimum number of times we need to append the last digit \\( d \\) to the number \\( N \\) to obtain a number divisible by 3 is twice. \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2 \\ \\text{times}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\n71. Let $a$ be a non-zero number in $p$-arithmetic. Prove that:\n\na) The multiplication scheme by $a$ decomposes into cycles;\n\nb) All these cycles (excluding the cycle containing 0) have the same length.\n\nDeduce from this that $a^{p-1}=1$.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove these two parts about multiplication by a non-zero element 'a' in p-arithmetic. First, that the multiplication scheme decomposes into cycles, and second, that all these cycles (except the one with 0) have the same length. Then, using that, I have to deduce that a^{p-1} = 1. Hmm, let me start by understanding what exactly is 