rations correspond to a spiral similarity transformation.\n\nHere's a rough proof sketch using complex numbers:\n\n1. Place the original triangle H\u2080 in the complex plane with P at the origin.\n\n2. Each pedal operation corresponds to multiplying the complex coordinates by a certain complex number and taking the conjugate. This is a bit hand-wavy, but suppose that projecting a point onto a line can be represented as a reflection followed by a scaling.\n\n3. After three such operations, the net effect is a multiplication by a complex number (scaling and rotation), hence a similarity.\n\nAlternatively, a more precise approach from triangle geometry:\n\nConsider that each pedal triangle construction with respect to point P involves orthocentric projections. The composition of three orthocentric projections can be shown to result in a spiral similarity.\n\nAnother angle: the pedal triangle operation is related to the isogonal conjugate. The isogonal conjugate of a point P with respect to triangle H\u2080 is another point Q such that the pedal triangle of Q is similar to the pedal triangle of P but rotated. After three iterations, this process might cycle back.\n\nHowever, the exact mechanics require deeper exploration. Given time constraints, I'll outline a formal proof using complex numbers and transformations.\n\n**Formal Proof Sketch:**\n\n1. **Complex Plane Setup:**\n   - Let\u2019s place point P at the origin in the complex plane for simplicity.\n   - Let H\u2080 have vertices A\u2080, B\u2080, C\u2080 represented as complex numbers.\n\n2. **Pedal Triangle Transformation:**\n   - The vertices of H\u2081 are the projections of P (origin) onto the sides of H\u2080.\n   - The projection of the origin onto the line through B\u2080 and C\u2080 is given by:\n     \\[\n     A\u2081 = \\frac{B\u2080 \\overline{C\u2080} - \\overline{B\u2080} C\u2080}{\\overline{C\u2080} - \\overline{B\u2080} + C\u2080 - B\u2080}\n     \\]\n     Similar expressions for B\u2081 and C\u208