e solution sets \\( P \\) (where \\( x > f(x) \\)) and \\( Q \\) (where \\( x > f(f(x)) \\)). \n\n1. **Subset Relation \\( P \\subseteq Q \\):**\n   - If \\( x \\in P \\), then \\( x > f(x) \\). Applying \\( f \\) (strictly increasing) to both sides: \\( f(x) > f(f(x)) \\). Combining, \\( x > f(x) > f(f(x)) \\), so \\( x > f(f(x)) \\). Thus, \\( x \\in Q \\), implying \\( P \\subseteq Q \\).\n\n2. **Subset Relation \\( Q \\subseteq P \\):**\n   - If \\( x \\in Q \\), then \\( x > f(f(x)) \\). Suppose \\( x \\leq f(x) \\). Applying \\( f \\) (strictly increasing) yields \\( f(x) \\leq f(f(x)) \\). Hence, \\( x \\leq f(x) \\leq f(f(x)) \\), contradicting \\( x > f(f(x)) \\). Therefore, \\( x > f(x) \\), so \\( x \\in P \\), implying \\( Q \\subseteq P \\).\n\nSince \\( P \\subseteq Q \\) and \\( Q \\subseteq P \\), we conclude \\( P = Q \\).\n\n**Answer:** B. \\( P = Q \\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Define the sets \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\)**\n\n   We start with:\n   - \\( P = \\{ x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\mid x > f(x) \\} \\)\n   - \\( Q = \\{ x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\mid x > f[f(x)] \\} \\)\n\n2. **Show \\(P \\subseteq Q \\)**\n\n   Take any \\( x_0 \\in P \\). This means \\( x_0 > f(x_0) \\).\n\n   Since \\( f \\) is strictly increasing, \\( f(x_0) > f(f(x_0)) \\). Hence, we get:\n   \\[\n   x_0 > f(x_0) > f(f(x_0)) \\implies x_0 > f(f(x_0))\n   \\]\n   Therefore, \\( x_0 \\in Q \\). This shows that \\( P \\subseteq Q \\).\n\n3. **Show \\( Q \\subseteq P \\)**\n\n   Take any \\( y_0 \\in Q \\). This means \\( y_0 > f(f(y_0)) \\).\n\n   We need to show \\( y_0 > f(y_0) \\). Suppose for contradiction that \\( y_0 \\leq f(y_0) \\).\n\n   Given that \\( f \\) is strictly increasing, applying \\( f \\) to both sides, we get:\n   \\[\n   f(y_0) \\leq f(f(y_0))\n   \\]\n   But we know:\n   \\[\n   f(f(y_0)) < y_0\n   \\]\n   Combining the inequalities:\n   \\[\n   f(y_0) \\leq f(f(y_0)) < y_0\n   \\]\n   Which contradicts our assumption \\( y_0 \\leq f(y_0) \\).\n\n   Thus, our 