e part c) is n(n+1)(n+2)(n+3)/something?\n\nWait, let's check term 1: 1/4. If we follow the pattern from part b), which was sum of 2/(n(n+1)(n+2)), maybe part c) is sum of 3/(n(n+1)(n+2)(n+3))? Let's check.\n\nFor n=1: 3/(1\u00d72\u00d73\u00d74) = 3/24 = 1/8. But the first term here is 1/4, so that doesn't match. Alternatively, maybe 6/(n(n+1)(n+2)(n+3)). For n=1: 6/24 = 1/4. Term 1: 1/4. Term 2: 6/(2\u00d73\u00d74\u00d75) = 6/120 = 1/20. Term 3: 6/(3\u00d74\u00d75\u00d76) = 6/360 = 1/60. Term 4: 6/(4\u00d75\u00d76\u00d77) = 6/840 = 1/140. Term 5: 6/(5\u00d76\u00d77\u00d78) = 6/1680 = 1/280. Perfect! So each term is 6/(n(n+1)(n+2)(n+3)). Therefore, the series is the sum from n=1 to infinity of 6/(n(n+1)(n+2)(n+3)).\n\nSo to prove that this sum equals 1/3. Let's use partial fractions again. Let's decompose 6/(n(n+1)(n+2)(n+3)).\n\nLet me denote the term as 6/(n(n+1)(n+2)(n+3)) = A/n + B/(n+1) + C/(n+2) + D/(n+3). Let's solve for A, B, C, D.\n\nMultiply both sides by n(n+1)(n+2)(n+3):\n\n6 = A(n+1)(n+2)(n+3) + Bn(n+2)(n+3) + Cn(n+1)(n+3) + Dn(n+1)(n+2).\n\nThis might be a bit tedious, but perhaps we can use specific values of n to find A, B, C, D.\n\nLet n = 0: 6 = A(1)(2)(3) + B(0) + C(0) + D(0) => 6 = 6A => A = 1.\n\nLet n = -1: 6 = A(0) + B(-1)(1)(2) + C(-1)(0)(2) + D(-1)(0)(1) => 6 = B(-1)(1)(2) => 6 = -2B => B = -3.\n\nLet n = -2: 6 = A(-1)(0)(1) + B(-2)(0)(1) + C(-2)(-1)(1) + D(-2)(-1)(0) => 6 = C(-2)(-1)(1) => 6 = 2C => C = 3.\n\nLet n = -3: 6 = A(-2)(-1)(0) + B(-3)(-1)(0) + C(-3)(-2)(0) + D(-3)(-2)(-1) => 6 = D(-3)(-2)(-1) => 6 = -6D => D = -1.\n\nTherefore, the decomposition is:\n\n1/n - 3/(n+1) + 3/(n+2) - 1/(n+3).\n\nSo the term becomes 1/n - 3/(n+1) + 3/(n+2) - 1/(n+3).\n\nThus, the series sum is the sum from n=1 to infinity of [1/n - 3/(n+1) + 3/(n+2) - 1/(n+3)]. Let's write out the terms:\n\nFor n=1: 1/1 - 3/2 + 3/3 - 1/4,\n\nn=2: 1/2 - 3/3 + 3/4 - 1/5,\n\nn=3: 1/3 - 3/4 + 3/5 - 1/6,\n\nn=4: 1/4 - 3/5 + 3/6 - 1/7,\n\n...\n\nLet's add these up term by term:\n\n1 - 3/2 + 3/3 - 1/4 + 1/2 - 3/3 + 3/4 - 1/5 + 1/3 - 3/4 + 3/5 - 1/6 + 1/4 - 3/5 + 3/6 - 1/7 + ...\n\nLet's group like terms:\n\n1 + (-3/2 + 1/2) + (3/3 - 3/3) + (-1/4 + 3/4 - 3/4) + (-1/5 + 3/5 - 3/5) + ... \n\nWait, let me track each term's coefficient:\n\nFor 1/n:\n\nn=1: 1,\n\nn=2: 1/2,\n\nn=3: 1/3,\n\nn=4: 1/4,\n\n...\n\nFor -3/(n+1):\n\nn=1: -3/2,\n\nn=2: -3/3,\n\nn=3: -3/4,\n\nn=4: -3/5,\n\n...\n\nFor 3/(n+2):\n\nn=1: 3/3,\n\nn=2: 3/4,\n\nn=3: 3/5,\n\nn=4: 3/6,\n\n...\n\nFor -1/(n+3):\n\nn=1: -1/4,\n\nn=2: -1/5,\n\nn=3: -1/6,\n\nn=4: -1/7,\n\n...\n\nSo let's reorganize the terms by denominator:\n\n1 (from 1/1),\n\n-3/2 + 1/2 (from n=1 and n=2 terms of -3/(n+1) and 1/n),\n\n3/3 - 3/3 (from n=1 and n=2 terms of 3/(n+2) and -3/(n+1)),\n\n-1/4 + 3/4 - 3/4 (from n=1, n=2, n=3 terms),\n\n-1/5 + 3/5 - 3/5 (from n=2, n=3, n=4 terms),\n\n... and so on.\n\nCalculating each group:\n\n1,\n\n(-3/2 + 1/2) = -1,\n\n(3/3 - 3/3) = 0,\n\n(-1/4 + 3/4 - 3/4) = (-1/4),\n\n(-1/5 + 3/5 - 3/5) = (-1/5),\n\n...\n\nSo the sum becomes 1 - 1 - 1/4 - 1/5 - 1/6 - ... Wait, that seems negative. But this can't be right because the original series is all positive terms summing to 1/3. Maybe I made a mistake in grouping.\n\nWait, perhaps a better way is to telescope. Let's write out the partial sum S_N:\n\nS_N = sum_{n=1}^N [1/n - 3/(n+1) + 3/(n+2) - 1/(n+3)]\n\n= sum_{n=1}^N 1/n - 3 sum_{n=1}^N 1/(n+1) + 3 sum_{n=1}^N 1/(n+2) - sum_{n=1}^N 1/(n+3)\n\n= [sum_{n=1}^N 1/n] - 3[sum_{n=2}^{N+1} 1/n] + 3[sum_{n=3}^{N+2} 1/n] - [sum_{n=4}^{N+3} 1/n]\n\nLet me denote H_N as the Nth harmonic number. Then:\n\n= H_N - 3(H_{N+1} - 1/1) + 3(H_{N+2} - 1/1 - 1/2) - (H_{N+3} - 1/1 - 1/2 - 1/3)\n\nWait, maybe expanding each sum:\n\nFirst term: sum_{n=1}^N 1/n = H_N\n\nSecond term: -3 sum_{n=2}^{N+1} 1/n = -3 (H_{N+1} - 1)\n\nThird term: +3 sum_{n=3}^{N+2} 1/n = +3 (H_{N+2} - H_2)\n\nFourth term: - sum_{n=4}^{N+3} 1/n = - (H_{N+3} - H_3)\n\nTherefore, S_N = H_N - 3(H_{N+1} - 1) + 3(H_{N+2} - H_2) - (H_{N+3} - H_3)\n\nLet me compute each term:\n\nFirst term: H_N\n\nSecond term: -3H_{N+1} + 3\n\nThird term: +3H_{N+2} - 3H_2\n\nFourth term: -H_{N+3} + H_3\n\nCombine all terms:\n\nH_N - 3H_{N+1} + 3 + 3H_{N+2} - 3H_2 - H_{N+3} + H_3\n\nRecall that H_{N+1} = H_N + 1/(N+1)\n\nSimilarly, H_{N+2} = H_N + 1/(N+1) + 1/(N+2)\n\nH_{N+3} = H_N + 1/(N+1) + 1/(N+2) + 1/(N+3)\n\nLet me substitute these into the expression:\n\nH_N - 3(H_N + 1/(N+1)) + 3 + 3(H_N + 1/(N+1) + 1/(N+2)) - 3H_2 - (H_N + 1/(N+1) + 1/(N+2) + 1/(N+3)) + H_3\n\nExpanding term by term:\n\n= H_N - 3H_N - 3/(N+1) + 3 + 3H_N + 3/(N+1) + 3/(N+2) - 3H_2 - H_N - 1/(N+1) - 1/(N+2) - 1/(N+3) + H_3\n\nNow, combine like terms:\n\nH_N - 3H_N + 3H_N - H_N = (1 - 3 + 3 - 1)H_N = 0\n\nTerms with 1/(N+1):\n\n-3/(N+1) + 3/(N+1) - 1/(N+1) = (-3 + 3 -1)/(N+1) = -1/(N+1)\n\nTerms with 1/(N+2):\n\n3/(N+2) - 1/(N+2) = 2/(N+2)\n\nTerms with 1/(N+3):\n\n-1/(N+3)\n\nConstants:\n\n3 - 3H_2 + H_3\n\nRecall that H_2 = 1 + 1/2 = 3/2,\n\nH_3 = 1 + 1/2 + 1/3 = 11/6.\n\nTherefore:\n\n3 - 3*(3/2) + 11/6 = 3 - 9/2 + 11/6 = Convert to sixths:\n\n18/6 - 27/6 + 11/6 = (18 - 27 + 11)/6 = 2/6 = 1/3.\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\nS_N = 0 - 1/(N+1) + 2/(N+2) - 1/(N+3) + 1/3.\n\nTherefore, as N approaches infinity, the terms -1/(N+1), 2/(N+2), -1/(N+3) all approach 0. Therefore, the limit of S_N as N\u2192\u221e is 0 + 0 + 0 + 1/3 = 1/3. Hence, the sum of the series is indeed 1/3. Therefore, part c) is proven.\n\nLet me just check the telescoping steps again for part c) to ensure I didn't make any arithmetic errors. The partial fraction decomposition gave 1/n - 3/(n+1) + 3/(n+2) - 1/(n+3), which when summed from n=1 to infinity, the partial sums involve cancellation of many terms, leading to the remaining terms tending to zero, leaving 1/3. Yes, that seems correct.\n\nSo all three equalities are proven using partial fractions and telescoping series.", "deepseek_solution": "a) The series \\( \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{n(n+1)} \\) telescopes. Each term decomposes as \\( \\frac{1}{n} - \\frac{1}{n+1} \\). Summing these cancels all intermediate terms, leaving \\( 1 - \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{n+1} = 1 \\).\n\n**Answer:**  \n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]\n\nb) The series \\( \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{2}{n(n+1)(n+2)} \\) decomposes into partial fractions \\( \\frac{1}{n} - \\frac{2}{n+1} + \\frac{1}{n+2} \\). Telescoping sums cancel terms, resulting in \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\).\n\n**Answer:**  \n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\]\n\nc) The series \\( \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{6}{n(n+1)(n+2)(n+3)} \\) decomposes into \\( \\frac{1}{n} - \\frac{3}{n+1} + \\frac{3}{n+2} - \\frac{1}{n+3} \\). Telescoping leaves only \\( \\frac{1}{3} \\) as \\( n \\to \\infty \\).\n\n**Answer:**  \n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n**Part (a)**\n\n1. Observe the given series: \n    \\[\n    1 = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{12} + \\frac{1}{20} + \\frac{1}{30} + \\ldots\n    \\]\n\n2. According to the Leibniz triangle property, the given sum of fractions can be unfolded step-by-step and summed:\n\n    - Recall the property of the Leibniz triangle:\n      \\[\n      \\frac{1}{n(n+1)} = \\frac{1}{n} - \\frac{1}{n+1}\n      \\]\n\n3. Start expanding the series:\n    \\[\n    1 = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\n    \\]\n\n    These first steps can be noted as:\n    \\[\n    1 = \\frac{1}{2} + \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)\n    \\]\n\n4. Continue this pattern by replacing each fraction according to the property:\n    \\[\n    1 = \\frac{1}{2} + \\left( \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{3} \\right)\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    1 = \\frac{1}{2} + \\left( \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{12} + \\frac{1}{4} \\right)\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    1 = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{12} + \\frac{1}{20} + \\frac{1}{5} + \\ldots\n    \\]\n\n5. Continuing this further would show that the series converges to 1 by continuously adding smaller and smaller fractions.\n\n**Conclusion for Part (a):**\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]\n\n**Part (b)**\n\n1. Observe the given series:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{12} + \\frac{1}{30} + \\frac{1}{60} + \\frac{1}{105} + \\ldots\n    \\]\n\n2. According to Leibniz triangle property, simplify term by term:\n\n    - Start by expanding:\n      \\[\n      \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{3} + \\left( \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{6} \\right)\n      \\]\n\n3. Further pattern replacement:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{3} + \\left( \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{12} \\right)\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{12} + \\left( \\frac{1}{30} - \\frac{1}{20} \\right)\n    \\]\n\n4. Following this pattern ultimately sums up fraction by fraction according to Leibniz triangle property rules.\n\n**Conclusion for Part (b):**\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\]\n\n**Part (c)**\n\n1. Observe the given series:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{20} + \\frac{1}{60} + \\frac{1}{140} + \\frac{1}{280} + \\ldots\n    \\]\n\n2. Simplify term by term using similar steps as the above properties:\n\n    - Start expanding:\n      \\[\n      \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{1}{4} + \\left( \\frac{1}{6} - \\frac{1}{20} \\right)\n      \\]\n\n3. Continue the pattern:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{20} + \\left( \\frac{1}{12} - \\frac{1}{60} \\right)\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{20} + \\frac{1}{60} + \\left( \\frac{1}{140} - \\frac{1}{105} \\right)\n    \\]\n\n4. Completing this pattern, each term nicely cancels out extra fractions to match the given terms.\n\n**Conclusion for Part (c):**\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "On a counter, there are 10 weights with masses \\( n, n+1, \\ldots, n+9 \\). A seller took one of them, after which the total weight of all the remaining weights was 1457. What is the weight of the weight that the seller took?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out this problem. So, there are 10 weights on a counter, each with masses n, n+1, n+2, ..., up to n+9. The seller took one of them, and the total weight of the remaining ones is 1457. We need to find out which weight the seller took. Hmm, okay.\n\nFirst, let me make sure I understand the problem correctly. There are 10 consecutive weights starting from some integer n, so their masses are n, n+1, n+2, ..., n+9. Then one of these is removed, and the sum of the remaining nine is 1457. We need to determine which one was removed. Got it.\n\nSo, the key here is probably to figure out what n is first, right? Because if we can find the value of n, then we can compute the total sum of all 10 weights, subtract 1457 from it, and the result should be the weight that was taken. That makes sense. Because the total sum minus the remaining sum equals the missing weight.\n\nSo, let's denote the total sum of all 10 weights as S. Then, S - (remaining sum) = missing weight. The remaining sum is given as 1457, so S - 1457 = missing weight. Therefore, if we can find S, which is the sum from n to n+9, then subtract 1457 to get the missing weight.\n\nBut to find S, we need to know n. So how do we find n? Well, let's think about the formula for the sum of consecutive numbers. The sum of an arithmetic sequence is given by (number of terms)/2 * (first term + last term). Here, there are 10 terms, starting at n and ending at n+9. Therefore, the sum S is:\n\nS = 10/2 * (n + (n + 9)) = 5 * (2n + 9) = 10n + 45.\n\nSo, S = 10n + 45. Then, the missing weight is S - 1457 = 10n + 45 - 1457 = 10n - 1412.\n\nBut the missing weight has to be one of the original weights: n, n+1, ..., n+9. Therefore, 10n - 1412 must equal one of these. Let's write that as an equation:\n\n10n - 1412 = n + k, where k is between 0 and 9 (inclusive).\n\nWait, that might not be the most straightforward way. Alternatively, since the missing weight is in the range [n, n+9], then 10n - 1412 must be within that range. So:\n\nn \u2264 10n - 1412 \u2264 n + 9\n\nLet's solve these inequalities:\n\nFirst inequality: n \u2264 10n - 1412\n\nSubtract n from both sides: 0 \u2264 9n - 1412\n\nAdd 1412 to both sides: 9n \u2265 1412\n\nDivide by 9: n \u2265 1412/9 \u2248 156.888...\n\nSince n must be an integer, n \u2265 157.\n\nSecond inequality: 10n - 1412 \u2264 n + 9\n\nSubtract n from both sides: 9n - 1412 \u2264 9\n\nAdd 1412 to both sides: 9n \u2264 1421\n\nDivide by 9: n \u2264 1421/9 \u2248 157.888...\n\nSince n must be an integer, n \u2264 157.\n\nWait, that's interesting. The first inequality gives n \u2265 157, and the second gives n \u2264 157. Therefore, n must be exactly 157.\n\nSo, n = 157. Then, the total sum S is 10*157 + 45 = 1570 + 45 = 1615. Therefore, the missing weight is 1615 - 1457 = 158.\n\nBut let's check if 158 is indeed one of the weights. The weights are from 157 to 166 (since n=157, n+9=166). So 158 is in that range, specifically n+1. Therefore, the missing weight is 158.\n\nWait, let me verify all steps again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nFirst, sum S is calculated as 10n + 45. Then missing weight is S - 1457 = 10n + 45 - 1457 = 10n - 1412. Then, setting up inequalities that 10n - 1412 is between n and n+9. Solving both inequalities gives n must be 157. Then, substituting back, missing weight is 10*157 -1412= 1570 -1412= 158. Since the weights are 157,158,...,166, 158 is indeed one of them.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 158. But let me check with another approach to be absolutely sure.\n\nAlternatively, the total sum of the weights is 10n + 45. If we remove one weight, say x, then the remaining sum is 10n + 45 - x = 1457. Therefore, x = 10n + 45 - 1457 = 10n - 1412. Since x must be one of n, n+1, ..., n+9, so x = n + k where k is between 0 and 9. Therefore:\n\n10n - 1412 = n + k\n\nTherefore, 9n = 1412 + k\n\nSo, n = (1412 + k)/9\n\nSince n must be an integer, 1412 + k must be divisible by 9. Let's compute 1412 divided by 9:\n\n1412 \u00f7 9: 9*156=1404, so 1412 -1404=8. So 1412 = 9*156 +8. Therefore, 1412 +k must be 9*(156 + m) where m is 0 or 1. So, k must be equal to 9*156 +9m -1412= 9m -8. Since k is between 0 and 9, let's solve 9m -8 \u2208 [0,9].\n\n9m -8 \u22650 \u21d2 m \u2265 8/9 \u22480.888 \u21d2 m=1,2,..., since m must be integer.\n\n9m -8 \u22649 \u21d29m \u226417\u21d2m \u226417/9\u22481.888\u21d2 m=1.\n\nTherefore, m=1. Then k=9*1 -8=1. Therefore, k=1. So, x= n +1. Then, n= (1412 +1)/9=1413/9=157. Therefore, n=157, missing weight x=157 +1=158. Same result. So, yes, that confirms it.\n\nAnother way: Let's compute 1412 modulo 9. Since 1412 \u00f79=156 with remainder 8. So 1412 \u22618 mod9. Then, 1412 +k \u22618 +k mod9. For n=(1412 +k)/9 to be integer, 8 +k \u22610 mod9. Therefore, k\u22611 mod9. Since k is from 0 to9, k=1. Therefore, x= n +1, and n=(1412 +1)/9=1413/9=157. Therefore, x=158. Same answer.\n\nSo, all approaches lead to the same conclusion: the missing weight is 158. Therefore, the answer is 158.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe weight the seller took is \\boxed{158}.", "deepseek_solution": "First, we recognize that the total weight of the 10 weights \\( n, n+1, \\ldots, n+9 \\) is given by the sum of an arithmetic sequence. The sum \\( S \\) of these 10 weights is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\nS = \\frac{10}{2} \\times (n + (n + 9)) = 5 \\times (2n + 9) = 10n + 45\n\\]\n\nAfter one weight is taken, the remaining total weight is 1457. Let \\( x \\) be the weight taken. Then:\n\n\\[\n10n + 45 - x = 1457\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\nx = 10n + 45 - 1457 = 10n - 1412\n\\]\n\nSince \\( x \\) must be one of the weights \\( n, n+1, \\ldots, n+9 \\), we set up the inequality:\n\n\\[\nn \\leq 10n - 1412 \\leq n + 9\n\\]\n\nSolving the inequalities:\n\n1. \\( n \\leq 10n - 1412 \\)\n   \\[\n   0 \\leq 9n - 1412 \\implies 9n \\geq 1412 \\implies n \\geq \\frac{1412}{9} \\approx 156.888\n   \\]\n   Since \\( n \\) is an integer, \\( n \\geq 157 \\).\n\n2. \\( 10n - 1412 \\leq n + 9 \\)\n   \\[\n   9n - 1412 \\leq 9 \\implies 9n \\leq 1421 \\implies n \\leq \\frac{1421}{9} \\approx 157.888\n   \\]\n   Since \\( n \\) is an integer, \\( n \\leq 157 \\).\n\nCombining both results, \\( n = 157 \\).\n\nSubstituting \\( n = 157 \\) back into the expression for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\nx = 10 \\times 157 - 1412 = 1570 - 1412 = 158\n\\]\n\nVerification confirms that 158 is within the range of weights from 157 to 166. Thus, the weight taken by the seller is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{158}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet's begin by setting up the problem. We are given 10 weights with masses \\( n, n+1, \\ldots, n+9 \\) and a total weight of 1457 grams after one weight is removed.\n\n1. **Calculate the total weight of all 10 weights**:\n\n   The sum of the weights \\( n, n+1, \\ldots, n+9 \\) forms an arithmetic series. The sum of an arithmetic series can be found using the formula:\n   \\[\n   S = \\frac{N}{2} \\left(2a + (N - 1)d \\right)\n   \\]\n   where \\( S \\) is the sum, \\( a \\) is the first term, \\( d \\) is the common difference, and \\( N \\) is the number of terms.\n\n   In this case:\n   - \\( N = 10 \\)\n   - \\( a = n \\)\n   - \\( d = 1 \\)\n\n   Therefore, the total weight of the 10 weights is:\n   \\[\n   S = \\frac{10}{2} \\left(2n + (10 - 1) \\cdot 1 \\right) = 5 \\left(2n + 9\\right) = 10n + 45\n   \\]\n\n2. **Relation between the total weight and remaining weight**:\n\n   Suppose the weight taken by the seller is \\( n + x \\), where \\( x \\) is an integer from 0 to 9.\n\n   The sum of the remaining weights is then given by:\n   \\[\n   10n + 45 - (n + x) = 1457\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplify the equation**:\n\n   \\[\n   10n + 45 - n - x = 1457 \\implies 9n + 45 - x = 1457\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve for \\( x \\)**:\n\n   Rearrange the equation to:\n   \\[\n   9n + 45 - x = 1457 \\implies 9n + 45 - 1457 = x \\implies 9n - 1412 = x\n   \\]\n\n   To find \\( x \\), we need to figure out \\( 9n \\) and check the remainder when \\( 1457 \\) is divided by \\( 9 \\):\n   \\[\n   1457 \\mod 9 = 161 \\cdot 9 + 8\n   \\]\n   So \\( 1457 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{9} \\).\n\n   Hence,\n   \\[\n   9n + 45 \\equiv x \\pmod{9} \\implies 0 \\equiv x \\pmod{9}\n   \\]\n   Since \\( x \\) ranges from 0 to 9, the only value \\( x \\) can be is \\( 1 \\).\n\n5. **Substitute \\( x = 1 \\)**:\n\n   \\[\n   9n + 45 - 1457 = 1 \\implies 9n = 1412 \\implies n = \\frac{1416}{9} = 157\n   \\]\n\n6. **Determine the weight taken**:\n\n   The weight taken by the seller is \\( n + x = 157 + 1 \\).\n\n### Conclusion\n\\[\n\\boxed{158}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\nA fast-food chain offers summer jobs to students with a salary of 25,000 rubles per month. Those who perform well receive an additional monthly bonus of 5,000 rubles.\n\nHow much will a well-performing student working at the fast-food chain earn per month (take-home pay) after income tax deduction?\n\nProvide only the number without units in the answer!", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's tackle this problem step by step. So, the question is about calculating the take-home pay for a well-performing student working at a fast-food chain after income tax deduction. The given information is a base salary of 25,000 rubles per month and a bonus of 5,000 rubles for good performance. I need to figure out the total earnings before tax, then subtract the income tax to get the final amount.\n\nFirst, I should confirm if there's any specific income tax rate mentioned. The problem states \"income tax deduction,\" but doesn't specify the rate. In many countries, income tax can vary, but since the question is about Russia (since the currency is rubles), I should recall what the standard income tax rate is there. From what I remember, Russia has a flat income tax rate for individuals, which is 13%. So, maybe that's the rate we need to apply here.\n\nLet me verify that. Yes, as of recent years, Russia's personal income tax is a flat 13% for most types of income. So, the tax would be 13% of the total income. \n\nNext, calculate the total gross income. The base salary is 25,000 rubles, and the bonus for good performance is 5,000 rubles. So, adding those together: 25,000 + 5,000 = 30,000 rubles per month before taxes.\n\nNow, apply the 13% income tax to this amount. To compute the tax, multiply 30,000 by 0.13. Let me do that calculation: 30,000 * 0.13 = 3,900 rubles. So, the tax deducted would be 3,900 rubles.\n\nSubtracting the tax from the gross income gives the take-home pay: 30,000 - 3,900 = 26,100 rubles.\n\nWait, but let me double-check these steps to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\nFirst, total earnings: 25k + 5k = 30k. Correct. Tax rate 13% on 30k: 30,000 * 0.13. Let's compute that again. 30,000 divided by 100 is 300, so 300 *13 = 3,900. Yes, that's correct. Then subtracting 3,900 from 30,000 gives 26,100. That seems right.\n\nIs there any chance that the bonus is taxed differently? In some tax systems, bonuses might be subject to different rates or calculations, but I think in Russia's flat tax system, all income is taxed at the same 13% rate regardless of whether it's salary, bonus, etc. So, the bonus would just be added to the base salary, and the total is taxed at 13%.\n\nTherefore, the calculation should be straightforward: total income is 30,000, tax is 13%, take-home pay is 26,100 rubles. That's the number I should provide.", "deepseek_solution": "26100", "ground_truth_solution": "1. Calculate the total monthly earnings of a student who works well in the \"Wings and Legs\" fast-food network.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total earnings} = \\text{Base salary} + \\text{Bonus} = 25000 \\, \\text{rubles} + 5000 \\, \\text{rubles}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total earnings} = 30000 \\, \\text{rubles}\n\\]\n\n2. Determine the amount of income tax to be deducted. The income tax rate is \\(13\\%\\).\n\n\\[\n\\text{Income tax} = 30000 \\, \\text{rubles} \\times 0.13\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{Income tax} = 3900 \\, \\text{ru