) and \\(T = b + d\\). Then the expression becomes:\n\n\\(E = S(1 - T) + 2T\\)\n\nSo, \\(E = S(1 - T) + 2T = S - ST + 2T = S + T(2 - S)\\)\n\nHmm, interesting. So now the expression is in terms of \\(S\\) and \\(T\\), where \\(S = a + c\\) and \\(T = b + d\\). But we have to remember that \\(a, c\\) are each in [-7.5, 7.5], so \\(S\\) can range from -15 to 15. Similarly, \\(T = b + d\\) can also range from -15 to 15.\n\nBut maybe this substitution isn't directly helpful. Let's think again. Alternatively, perhaps I can consider the expression as a function of each variable and try to maximize step by step. For example, fix some variables and optimize others.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the method of completing the square or looking for quadratic terms. Let's see:\n\nLooking at the expression:\n\n\\(E = a + 2b + c + 2d - ab - bc - cd - da\\)\n\nLet me group the terms involving each variable:\n\nFor variable \\(a\\):\n\n\\( -ab - da + a = a(1 - b - d)\\)\n\nFor variable \\(b\\):\n\n\\(2b - ab - bc = b(2 - a - c)\\)\n\nFor variable \\(c\\):\n\n\\(c - bc - cd = c(1 - b - d)\\)\n\nFor variable \\(d\\):\n\n\\(2d - cd - da = d(2 - c - a)\\)\n\nWait, so each variable is multiplied by a term that's linear in other variables. This might suggest that we can use some strategy where we fix certain variables and optimize others.\n\nAlternatively, maybe take partial derivatives with respect to each variable and set them to zero to find critical points.\n\nLet's try that. Let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\nFor variable \\(a\\):\n\n\\(\\frac{\\partial E}{\\partial a} = 1 - b - d\\)\n\nFor variable \\(b\\):\n\n\\(\\frac{\\partial E}{\\partial b} = 2 - a - c\\)\n\nFor variable \\(c\\):\n\n\\(\\frac{\\partial E}{\\partial c} = 1 - b - d\\)\n\nFor variable \\(d\\):\n\n\\(\\frac{\\partial E}{\\partial d} = 2 - c - a\\)\n\nSetting these partial derivatives to zero gives the system of equations:\n\n1. \\(1 - b - d = 0\\) --> \\(b + d = 1\\)\n2. \\(2 - a - c = 0\\) --> \\(a + c = 2\\)