qual (PA=DA=1), so it's isosceles. So yes, this point also satisfies all four conditions.\n\nSo each pair of adjacent circles (A and B, B and C, C and D, D and A) intersect at two points each, giving points above and below the square. But since there are four pairs, but each intersection is shared between two pairs, perhaps leading to 8 points. But let's check.\n\nFor example, circles at A and B intersect at (0.5, sqrt(3)/2) and (0.5, -sqrt(3)/2). Similarly, circles at B and C would intersect at (1 + sqrt(3)/2, 0.5) and (1 - sqrt(3)/2, 0.5). Wait, but wait, if the square is from (0,0) to (1,1), then the circle centered at B(1,0) with radius 1 and circle centered at C(1,1) with radius 1 would intersect. Let's compute that.\n\nCircle B: (x-1)^2 + (y)^2 =1\n\nCircle C: (x-1)^2 + (y-1)^2 =1\n\nSubtracting the two equations:\n\n(y)^2 - (y-1)^2 =0 \u2192 y\u00b2 - (y\u00b2 -2y +1) =0 \u2192 2y -1=0 \u2192 y=0.5\n\nThen substituting back into circle B: (x-1)^2 + (0.5)^2 =1 \u2192 (x-1)^2 = 0.75 \u2192 x-1=\u00b1sqrt(0.75)=\u00b1(\u221a3)/2\u22480.866\n\nThus x=1\u00b1\u221a3/2\u22481\u00b10.866. So x\u22481.866 or x\u22480.134. But since the square is from x=0 to 1, x=1.866 is outside the square, and x=0.134 is inside. Wait, but coordinates can be outside the square. The problem allows P to be anywhere in the plane.\n\nSo the intersection points are (1 + \u221a3/2, 0.5) and (1 - \u221a3/2, 0.5). Similarly, circles centered at C and D will intersect at (0.5,1 + \u221a3/2) and (0.5,1 - \u221a3/2). Circles centered at D and A will intersect at (-\u221a3/2, 0.5) and (\u221a3/2, 0.5). Wait, but D is at (0,1), so circle centered at D(0,1) radius 1: equation x\u00b2 + (y-1)^2=1. Circle centered at A(0,0) radius 1: x\u00b2 + y\u00b2=1. Intersection points:\n\nSubtracting the equations:\n\nx\u00b2 + y\u00b2 - [x\u00b2 + (y-1)^2] =0 \u2192 y\u00b2 - (y\u00b2 -2y +1)=0 \u21922y -1=0\u2192 y=0.5\n\nThen substituting back into x\u00b2 + y\u00b2=1: x\u00b2 +0.25=1\u2192x\u00b2=0.75\u219