 being tangent to the circle, yielding unique solutions. Therefore, the values of \\( a \\) are \\(\\boxed{3}\\) and \\(\\boxed{-1}\\), with corresponding unique solutions \\(\\boxed{(-2, 1)}\\) and \\(\\boxed{(0, -1)}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "Given the system of inequalities:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx^{2} + y^{2} + 2x \\leq 1 \\\\\nx - y + a = 0 \\\\\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nwe are asked to find the value of \\(a\\) such that the system has a unique solution.\n\n1. From the second inequality \\( x - y + a = 0 \\), we can express \\( y \\) in terms of \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   y = x + a\n   \\]\n\n2. Substitute \\( y = x + a \\) into the first inequality \\( x^{2} + y^{2} + 2x \\leq 1 \\):\n   \\[\n   x^{2} + (x + a)^{2} + 2x \\leq 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x^{2} + x^{2} + 2ax + a^{2} + 2x \\leq 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2x^{2} + 2ax + 2x + a^{2} \\leq 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2x^{2} + 2(a + 1)x + a^{2} - 1 \\leq 0\n   \\]\n\n3. Let \\( f(x) = 2x^{2} + 2(a + 1)x + a^{2} - 1 \\). To have a unique solution for \\( f(x) \\leq 0 \\), the quadratic equation \\( f(x) = 0 \\) must have exactly one real root. This occurs when the discriminant \\( \\Delta \\) is zero.\n\n4. Calculate the discriminant \\( \\Delta \\) of the quadratic equation \\( 2x^{2} + 2(a + 1)x + (a^{2} - 1) = 0 \\):\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = [2(a + 1)]^{2} - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (a^{2} - 1)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = 4(a + 1)^{2} - 8(a^{2} - 1)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = 4(a^{2} + 2a + 1) - 8a^{2} + 8\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = 4a^{2} + 8a + 4 - 8a^{2} + 8\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = -4a^{2} + 8a + 12\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = 4(3 - a^{2} + 2a)\n   \\]\n   Set \\(\\Delta = 0\\):\n   \\[\n   4(-a^{2} + 2a + 3) = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -a^{2} + 2a + 3 = 0\n   \\]\n   Solve the quadratic equation:\n   \\[\n   a^{2} - 2a - 3 = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   (a - 3)(a + 1) = 0\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the solutions are:\n   \\[\n   a = 3 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad a = -1\n   \\]\n\n5. Verify the solutions:\n\n   - When \\( a = 3 \\):\n     \\[\n     y = x + 3\n     \\]\n     Substitute into the inequality:\n     \\[\n     2x^{2} + 2(3 + 1)x + 3^{2} - 1 \\leq 0\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2x^{2} + 8x + 9 - 1 \\leq 0\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2x^{2} + 8x + 8 \\leq 0\n     \\]\n     Simplify:\n     \\[\n     (x + 2)^{2} \\leq 0\n     \\]\n     Therefore, \\( x = -2 \\). From \\( y = x + 3 \\),\n     \\[\n     y = -2 + 3 = 1\n     \\]\n     The unique solution is:\n     \\[\n     (x, y) = (-2, 1)\n     \\]\n\n   - When \\( a = -1 \\):\n     \\[\n     y = x - 1\n     \\]\n     Substitute into the inequality:\n     \\[\n     2x^{2} + 2(-1 + 1)x + (-1)^{2} - 1 \\leq 0\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2x^{2} \\leq 0\n     \\]\n     Therefore, \\( x = 0 \\). From \\( y = x - 1 \\),\n     \\[\n     y = 0 - 1 = -1\n     \\]\n     The unique solution is:\n     \\[\n     (x, y) = (0, -1)\n     \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nTherefore, for \\( a = 3 \\), the unique solution is \\( (x, y) = (-2, 1) \\), and for \\( a = -1 \\), the unique solution is \\( (x, y) = (0, -1) \\).\n\nThe values of \\(a\\) that allow the system to have a unique solution are \n\\[\n\\boxed{3 \\text{ and } -1}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A rectangle is divided by two straight lines into four smaller rectangles with perimeters of 6, 10, and 12 (the rectangle with a perimeter of 10 shares sides with the rectangles with perimeters of 6 and 12). Find the perimeter of the fourth rectangle.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the perimeter of the fourth rectangle in a larger rectangle that's divided by two straight lines. The given perimeters are 6, 10, and 12. The rectangle with perimeter 10 shares sides with both the 6 and 12 ones. Let me try to visualize this first.\n\nLet me imagine the big rectangle divided into four smaller ones by two lines: one horizontal and one vertical. Let's say the vertical line divides the big rectangle into left and right parts, a