ight)\\).\n   - \\(F\\) is at \\(\\left(\\frac{11}{2}, \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\sqrt{6}\\right)\\).\n\n3. **Vectors \\( \\overrightarrow{DE} \\) and \\( \\overrightarrow{DF} \\):**\n   - \\( \\overrightarrow{DE} = \\left(-2, -3\\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{6}\\right) \\)\n   - \\( \\overrightarrow{DF} = \\left(2, -3\\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{6}\\right) \\)\n\n4. **Cross product \\( \\overrightarrow{DE} \\times \\overrightarrow{DF} \\):**\n   - Resulting cross product: \\( \\left(0, 4\\sqrt{6}, 12\\sqrt{3}\\right) \\)\n   - Magnitude: \\( \\sqrt{(4\\sqrt{6})^2 + (12\\sqrt{3})^2} = 4\\sqrt{33} \\)\n\n5. **Area of \\( \\triangle DEF \\):**\n   - Using the cross product magnitude: \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\times 4\\sqrt{33} = 2\\sqrt{33} \\)\n\nThus, the area of \\( \\triangle DEF \\) is \\(\\boxed{2\\sqrt{33}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Analyzing Geometric Relationships**:\n   - We are given a regular tetrahedron \\(ABCD\\), where all the faces are equilateral triangles.\n   - Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on edges \\(AB\\) and \\(AC\\) respectively.\n   - Given conditions: \\(BE = 3\\) and \\(EF = 4\\).\n\n2. **Determining Parallelism and Lengths**:\n   - Since \\(EF \\parallel\\) plane \\(BCD\\), it follows that \\(EF \\parallel BC\\).\n   - Also, due to the regular tetrahedron\u2019s symmetry, segment \\(EF\\) is parallel to one of its edges in the plane \\(BC\\).\n\n3. **Using the Equilateral Triangle Properties**:\n   - Each side of a face of the regular tetrahedron is of equal length.\n   - Given that \\(A, B, C, D\\) form a regular tetrahedron, all edge lengths are equal.\n\n4. **Calculating the Lengths of Edges**:\n   - Since \\(E\\) is on \\(AB\\), and each edge \\(AB = AD = a\\):\n   \\[\n   BE = 3 \\implies AE = AB - BE = a - 3.\n   \\]\n   - Point \\(F\\) lies on \\(AC\\), making \\(AF = a - 4\\).\n\n5. **Using the Property of the Tetrahedron**:\n   - Since \\(EF \\parallel BC\\), this implies a symmetry and thus \\(A E = AF\\). Hence,\n   \\[\n   AE = AF = 4.\n   \\]\n   