\( DK = \\frac{3a}{2} \\)\n     - \\( OD = R \\)\n     - \\( OM = x \\)\n     - \\( KM = \\frac{a\\sqrt{3}}{6} \\)\n   \n5. **Projecting and Using Pythagorean Theorem in a Different Triangle**:\n   By dropping a perpendicular \\( ON \\) from \\( O \\) to \\( DK \\), in the right triangle \\( DON \\):\n\n   \\[\n   R^2 = ON^2 + DN^2\n   \\]\n\n   - Since \\( DN = DK - KN \\):\n   \n     \\[\n     DN = DK - KM = \\frac{3a}{2} - x\n     \\]\n\n   - Hence, \\( ON = KM \\):\n   \n     \\[\n     ON = KM = \\frac{a\\sqrt{3}}{6}\n     \\]\n\n   - Now we write the Pythagorean theorem for \\( DON \\):\n\n     \\[\n     R^2 = \\left(\\frac{a\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{3a}{2} - x\\right)^2\n     \\]\n\n6. **Combining the Two Equations for \\( R^2 \\)**:\n   \n   Using the two equations for \\( R^2 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   R^2 = \\left(\\frac{a\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)^2 + x^2 = \\left(\\frac{a\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{3a}{2} - x\\right)^2\n   \\]\n\n7. **Solve for \\( x \\)**:\n   - Simplifying and equating coefficients, we solve for \\( x \\):\n\n     \\[\n     \\left(\\frac{a\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)^2 + x^2 = \\left(\\frac{a\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{3a}{2} - x\\right)^2\n     \\]\n\n   - Simplifying both sides:\n\n     \\[\n     \\frac{a^2}{3} + x^2 = \\frac{a^2}{12} + \\left(\\frac{3a}{2} - x\\right)^2\n     \\]\n\n   - Continue simplification to find \\( x \\):\n\n     \\[\n     \\frac{a^2}{3} + x^2 = \\frac{a^2}{12} + \\left(\\frac{9a^2}{4} - 3ax + x^2\\right)\n     \\]\n\n     \\[\n     \\frac{a^2}{3} + x^2 = \\frac{a^2}{12} + \\frac{9a^2}{4} - 3ax + x^2\n     \\]\n\n     \\[\n     \\frac{a^2}{3} = \\frac{a^2}{12} + \\frac{9a^2}{4} - 3ax\n     \\]\n\n     \\[\n     4 \\cdot \\frac{a^2}{3} = \\frac{a^2}{12} + \\frac{9a^2}{4} - 3ax\n     \\]\n\n     \\[\n     \\frac{4a^2}{3} = \\frac{a^2}{12} + \\frac{9a^2}{4} - 3ax\n     \\]\n\n     Multiply everything by 12 to clear fractions:\n\n     \\[\n     16a^2 = a^2 + 27a^2 - 36ax\n     \\]\n\n     \\[\n 