 3: x \u2265 1\n\nFor each case, I will rewrite the equation without absolute values and solve for x. Then, I need to check if the solutions obtained in each case lie within the interval of the case. If they do, they are valid solutions; if not, they are discarded. Finally, I will sum all valid real roots.\n\nStarting with Case 1: x < -3\n\nIn this interval, x + 3 < 0 (since x is less than -3) and x - 1 < 0 (since x is less than 1). Therefore, |x + 3| = -(x + 3) = -x - 3 and |x - 1| = -(x - 1) = -x + 1. Substituting these into the original equation:\n\n- x - 3 - (-x + 1) = x + 1\n\nSimplify the left side:\n\n- x - 3 + x - 1 = x + 1\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n(-x + x) + (-3 - 1) = x + 1\n\n0 - 4 = x + 1\n\n-4 = x + 1\n\nSubtract 1 from both sides:\n\nx = -5\n\nNow, check if x = -5 is in the interval x < -3. Since -5 < -3, this is a valid solution for Case 1.\n\nMoving on to Case 2: -3 \u2264 x < 1\n\nIn this interval, x + 3 \u2265 0 (since x is at least -3) and x - 1 < 0 (since x is less than 1). Therefore, |x + 3| = x + 3 and |x - 1| = -(x - 1) = -x + 1. Substitute into the equation:\n\n(x + 3) - (-x + 1) = x + 1\n\nSimplify left side:\n\nx + 3 + x - 1 = x + 1\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n2x + 2 = x + 1\n\nSubtract x and subtract 2 from both sides:\n\n2x + 2 - x - 2 = x + 1 - x - 2\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\nx = -1\n\nCheck if x = -1 is in the interval -3 \u2264 x < 1. Since -1 is between -3 and 1, this is a valid solution for Case 2.\n\nNow, Case 3: x \u2265 1\n\nIn this interval, both x + 3 \u2265 0 (since x \u2265 1, so x + 3 \u2265 4) and x - 1 \u2265 0. Therefore, |x + 3| = x + 3 and |x - 1| = x - 1. Substitute into the equation:\n\n(x + 3) - (x - 1) = x + 1\n\nSimplify left side:\n\nx + 3 - x + 1 = x + 1\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n4 = x + 1\n\nSubtract 1 from both sides:\n\nx = 3\n\nCheck if x = 3 is in the interval x \u2265 1. Yes, 3 is greater than 1, so this is a valid solution for Case 3.\n\nSo, the solutions are x = -5, x = -1, and x = 3. Wait, but hold on. Let me verify each solution in the original equation to ensure they are correct. Sometimes when dealing with absolute values, even if the solution is in the interval, substituting back can reveal extraneous solutions.\n\nFirst, check x = -5:\n\nLeft side: | -5 + 3 | - | -5 - 1 | = | -2 | - | -6 | = 2 - 6 = -4\n\nRight side: -5 + 1 = -4\n\nSo, -4 = -4. Correct.\n\nNext, check x = -1:\n\nLeft side: | -1 + 3 | - | -1 - 1 | = | 2 | - | -2 | = 2 - 2 = 0\n\nRight side: -1 + 1 = 0\n\n0 = 0. Correct.\n\nCheck x = 3:\n\nLeft side: |3 + 3| - |3 - 1| = |6| - |2| = 6 - 2 = 4\n\nRight side: 3 + 1 = 4\n\n4 = 4. Correct.\n\nSo all three solutions are valid. Wait, but hold on. The problem says \"the sum of all real roots\". So adding them up: -5 + (-1) + 3 = (-5 -1) +3 = -6 +3 = -3. But before I conclude, let me check if all these roots are indeed distinct and there are no duplicates or overlaps in the cases.\n\nWait, in the Case 1: x < -3 gives x=-5. Case 2: -3 \u2264x <1 gives x=-1. Case 3: x\u22651 gives x=3. These intervals don't overlap, so all three solutions are distinct. But let me check if there's another solution when x is exactly at the critical points, x=-3 or x=1. Maybe I should check these points separately.\n\nWait, when I set up the cases, for x < -3, -3 \u2264x <1, and x\u22651. The points x=-3 and x=1 are included in their respective intervals. Let me check x=-3:\n\nSubstitute x=-3 into original equation:\n\nLeft side: | -3 + 3 | - | -3 -1 | = |0| - | -4 | = 0 -4 = -4\n\nRight side: -3 +1 = -2\n\nSo, -4 \u2260 -2. Therefore, x=-3 is not a solution. Similarly, check x=1:\n\nLeft side: |1 +3| - |1 -1| = |4| - |0| =4 -0=4\n\nRight side:1 +1=2\n\n4\u22602. So x=1 is not a solution. Thus, the only solutions are x=-5, x=-1, and x=3.\n\nWait a minute, but the problem is asking for the sum of all real roots. So, -5 + (-1) +3= -3. But let me think again. Is there a possibility that I have considered too many cases? Let me confirm once more.\n\nOriginal equation: |x +3| - |x 