ty *regardless of leap years*, use the larger value (**367 people**) to account for 366 possible days.  \n   - However, most standard problems assume a **365-day year**, making **366 people** the typical answer.  \n\n### Final Answer:\n**366 people** are needed to ensure at least two share a birthday in a non-leap year. If leap years are explicitly included, the number becomes 367. Since the problem does not specify leap years, the standard answer is:  \n**366**.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Let's consider that there are \\(365\\) days in a year.\n2. We must determine the number of people required to ensure that at least two of them share the same birthday.\n\nWe can use the pigeonhole principle here. The pigeonhole principle states that if more objects (pigeons) are placed into fewer containers (pigeonholes) than there are objects, then at least one container must hold more than one object.\n\n3. In this context:\n   - The \"pigeonholes\" are the days of the year, i.e., \\(365\\) days.\n   - The \"pigeons\" are the people.\n\n4. According to the pigeonhole principle, if we have \\(365\\) people, it is possible that each person has a birthday on a different day, filling each of the \\(365\\) days exactly once.\n\n5. To guarantee that there is at least one shared birthday, an additional person must be included. This is because the \\(366\\)-th person must share a birthday with one of the people already counted (since all \\(365\\) days are already occupied).\n\nSo, the minimum number of people required to ensure at least two people share a birthday is:\n\n\\[\n365 + 1 = 366\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{366}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\nThe polynomial \\( G(x) \\) with real coefficients takes the value 2022 at exactly five different points \\( x_{1} < x_{2} < x_{3} < x_{4} < x_{5} \\). It is known that the graph of the function \\( y=G(x) \\) is symmetric with respect to the li