 can be written as (x/b) + (y/a) = 1, since it's the line connecting (b,0) and (0,a).\n\nSo, any point K on AB can be written as (x, y) where (x/b) + (y/a) = 1. And the distance from C (0,0) to K (x,y) is sqrt(x^2 + y^2) = a. So, sqrt(x^2 + y^2) = a. So, x^2 + y^2 = a^2.\n\nBut since (x/b) + (y/a) = 1, we can write y = a(1 - x/b). Substitute into x^2 + y^2 = a^2:\n\nx^2 + [a(1 - x/b)]^2 = a^2\n\nExpand that:\n\nx^2 + a^2(1 - 2x/b + x^2/b^2) = a^2\n\nMultiply out:\n\nx^2 + a^2 - 2a^2x/b + (a^2x^2)/b^2 = a^2\n\nSubtract a^2 from both sides:\n\nx^2 - 2a^2x/b + (a^2x^2)/b^2 = 0\n\nFactor x:\n\nx [x - 2a^2/b + (a^2x)/b^2] = 0\n\nWait, that seems messy. Maybe better to collect like terms:\n\nx^2 + (a^2x^2)/b^2 - 2a^2x/b = 0\n\nFactor x^2:\n\nx^2 [1 + a^2/b^2] - 2a^2x/b = 0\n\nLet me write this as:\n\nx^2 ( (b^2 + a^2)/b^2 ) - 2a^2x/b = 0\n\nMultiply through by b^2 to eliminate denominators:\n\nx^2 (b^2 + a^2) - 2a^2x b = 0\n\nFactor x:\n\nx [ x(b^2 + a^2) - 2a^2b ] = 0\n\nSo, solutions are x=0 or x(b^2 + a^2) - 2a^2b = 0\n\nBut x=0 would correspond to point B (0,a), but at x=0, y=a. However, the distance from C to B is sqrt(0 + a^2) = a, so point B is indeed one solution. But we need the other point K on AB such that CK = a. So, x = (2a^2b)/(b^2 + a^2)\n\nTherefore, coordinates of K are x = (2a^2b)/(b^2 + a^2) and y = a(1 - x/b) = a[1 - (2a^2b)/(b(b^2 + a^2)))] = a[1 - (2a^2)/(b^2 + a^2)] = a[ (b^2 + a^2 - 2a^2)/(b^2 + a^2) ) ] = a[ (b^2 - a^2)/(b^2 + a^2) )\n\nSo, coordinates of K are:\n\n( (2a^2b)/(a^2 + b^2), a(b^2 - a^2)/(a^2 + b^2) )\n\nNow, next part is about the angle bisector AL. AL is the bisector of angle A, so it should meet BC at point L. The angle bisector theorem states that BL/LC = AB/AC. Wait, in triangle ABC, the angle bisector from A to BC divides BC into segments proportional to AB and AC.\n\nWait, angle bisector theorem: In triangle ABC, if AL is the bisector of angle A, then BL / LC = AB / AC.\n\nGiven that ABC is a right triangle with right angle at 